WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.100
We are going to be speaking with Stephanie
Kelton, who's a professor of public policy

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:09.009
and economics at Stony Brook University and
one of the world's leading experts in modern

00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:14.849
monetary theory, unfortunately, due to a technical
issue, as I would call it.

00:00:14.849 --> 00:00:17.790
We lost the first bit of the conversation
with her.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:22.790
So I'm introducing her to you now and we're
going to join the conversation in progress.

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:27.760
If if Mitch McConnell and the Republicans
have decided that, you know, the four bills

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.710
that have been passed so far, that's enough.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:30.789
That's sufficient.

00:00:30.789 --> 00:00:32.700
We're going to close up and go home.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.540
We're not going to do anything that's going
to add to the deficit further.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.219
We're beginning to worry about the debt.

00:00:37.219 --> 00:00:40.350
And you're going to see state and local governments
out to dry.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:42.410
You're going to you know, there's a number
of other things.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:47.319
But I have to tell you, that's the one that
concerns me the most, because you're already

00:00:47.319 --> 00:00:53.249
hearing governors tell you they're very candid
about what the consequences will be if they

00:00:53.249 --> 00:00:56.280
don't get aid from the federal government.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:01.729
They're going to start cutting the public
workforce, firefighters, teachers, police

00:01:01.729 --> 00:01:04.510
officers, essential services.

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:07.040
Everything is going to be cut and it's going
to be cut deeply.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.610
They're going to be 20 percent, 30 percent
cuts, more lay offs.

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:17.080
And so what Congress is trying to do to put
some kind of a floor under the slowdown and,

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:21.750
you know, to provide some support for this
economy is going to be completely undone.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:27.150
When you get 50 little Herbert Hoover's running
around all over this country, working with

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.490
their state legislatures to slash budgets.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:34.520
So now along those lines, we the unemployment
insurance system.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:38.240
These are state systems which are being there
independently operated.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:40.530
Some you know, you file by phone, some Web
site.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:45.000
The systems are sort of functioning to different
degrees, depending on how overwhelmed and

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.220
modern they are.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:51.430
We've got the federal supplements for unemployment
insurance of up to six hundred dollars a week.

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:56.240
We're starting to hear that there are state
governors saying we can only really maintain

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:02.300
the state benefits for so long before those
run out, as they say.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:06.200
What is your perspective on what needs to
be done in that sense?

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.789
Well, a couple of things.

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:13.700
The the mistakes have already been made in
the sense that once you allow people to become

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.170
unemployed, then the way that you're left
to deal with them is through unemployment

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:19.819
insurance.

00:02:19.819 --> 00:02:24.920
And the the thing that happened after the
Great Recession, you probably remember, is

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.910
that people who went on unemployment insurance
then had the unemployment insurance.

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:32.030
That period of time runs out and then it expires.

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:33.130
And now you don't have anything.

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:37.300
And then you have to go back and Congress
has to fight and negotiate and pass a small

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:38.300
extension.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.300
And then it turns out not to be long enough.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:44.700
And you got all these people who became long
term unemployed, 26 weeks and more.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:48.640
Many people were jobless for a year or more
in that environment.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.360
And the benefits run out and all of the associated
problems.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.360
So it would have been.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:57.490
I just want to say I would have been much,
much better to have an effective paycheck

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:04.990
protection program in place that allowed employers
to keep hold of their workers, keep them on

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:11.230
payroll, avoid letting them slide into unemployment,
and then have this system, which as you describe,

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:12.490
is antiquated.

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:17.530
You've got people who are reporting that they've
made a thousand phone calls a day to try to

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.700
apply to get in the queue for unemployment
insurance.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.220
So you know what happens when it runs out?

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:31.650
Well, I guess we we hope for the best in terms
of Congress being willing to extend unemployment

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:37.540
insurance as needed or people are just going
to be left with with no income at all.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:39.920
And that's fundamentally I mean, that's how
it would have to be managed, right?

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:45.080
It would have to be managed where the Treasury
says we are going to fund these state programs

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:47.890
or we will make up anything that the states
are unable to pay.

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:49.000
That's the mechanism.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.000
Right.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.120
And as we've just been.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:52.120
Yeah.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:53.120
That's exactly right.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:57.470
If what you have is a situation where the
federal government sits in a unique position

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:01.890
as the issuer of the currency, it can't run
out of money.

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:05.930
State and local governments, completely different
state local governments can't issue the U.S.

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:06.930
dollar.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:11.560
They are users of the currency, which is why
every mayor and every governor essentially

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:17.010
is pleading with the federal government to
come through with grants, with aid to help

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:20.630
state and local governments because their
budgets don't work like the federal budget.

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:21.820
And so that's exactly right.

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:27.580
In an environment where revenues are collapsing
at the state and local level and state, local

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:33.010
governments are dependent on tax revenue to
pay the bills, whereas the federal government

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:36.950
is in, the federal government's going to have
to step up and write the check or we're going

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:42.950
to end up in a situation where you have mass
layoffs across the country, state public sector

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:49.669
workers and essentially states that are unable
to bear the burden of supporting the unemployment

00:04:49.669 --> 00:04:50.940
insurance system.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:54.050
I read a paper yesterday that argues that.

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:58.840
This is actually going to be much worse than
last, much longer than certainly the administration

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:02.770
is willing to admit when they say it's going
to go up like a rocket.

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:06.180
Analogies to Great Depression number two.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:12.340
My question to you is, what are the indicators
we should be paying attention to to determine

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:16.750
whether that's the direction we're going or
whether in six or eight months there could

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:21.650
be a significant portion of the losses recovered.

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:23.590
I mean, I think we're watching the unemployment
rate.

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:30.470
I think that, you know, you you can extend
loans and say that they turn into grants,

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:38.050
but you're still we are still relying on businesses
to apply for these loans to take some risk

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:41.010
because it doesn't cover all of their fixed
operating costs.

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:46.911
We're essentially saying, you know, do us
a favor, help us keep people on payroll, but

00:05:46.911 --> 00:05:50.780
we're going to cover some of your fixed costs,
but not all of your fixed costs through this

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:52.180
profit, through this process.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:56.670
So businesses that have let a lease, they
have rent, they have utilities, they have

00:05:56.670 --> 00:06:01.080
other fixed costs that they can't cover because
they're shut down.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:02.790
They don't have any cash flow.

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:03.790
Right.

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:04.790
They have no revenue.

00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:08.120
They're not going to take those loans and
keep the workers on payroll.

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:13.190
If it means they also have to incur a bunch
of other costs that won't be covered.

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:15.229
That's going to come out of their bottom line.

00:06:15.229 --> 00:06:20.780
So watch the unemployment rate and watch how
many of these businesses are essentially going

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:21.790
to be gone forever.

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:23.380
And that's the really scary thing.

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:28.850
Small businesses really are the backbone of
our economy in terms of, you know, supporting

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:29.850
jobs.

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:32.650
And, you know, I I've talked with people.

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:37.330
I got a friend out in Kansas City and he said
a few weeks ago, you could drive around and

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:42.699
you could see signs in the business storefronts
that said temporarily closed.

00:06:42.699 --> 00:06:43.699
Right.

00:06:43.699 --> 00:06:47.080
He went back a few days ago and he said the
signs no longer say that.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:50.990
They just say out of business, you know, they're
boarding up, they're going under.

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:56.240
So I think those are the kind of indicators
that let you know that the kind of thing that

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:03.639
you just said, that this could be orders of
magnitude worse than the Great Recession and

00:07:03.639 --> 00:07:09.300
we could end up with an economy that is deeply
depressed for a long, long period of time.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:10.300
Yeah.

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:14.060
Here in Boston, we've seen a shift from open
for takeout and delivery to closed until further

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:16.370
notice, which is sort of another since that.

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:19.430
Couple other things just to talk briefly about
before I let you go.

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:23.620
One is the stock market looking at the level
of the Dow?

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:26.470
Tell us anything relevant right now.

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:29.920
Well, it tells you a little bit.

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:34.370
I suppose it tells you that investors feel.

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:37.070
Let OK.

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:38.420
OK.

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:45.789
About the degree of commitment on the part
of the Fed to help maintain stock market prices.

00:07:45.789 --> 00:07:52.889
I mean, I think it's a reflection of not so
much fundamentals, what we'd call economic

00:07:52.889 --> 00:07:59.199
fundamentals and the health of our firms and
the likelihood of a solid recovery in the

00:07:59.199 --> 00:08:01.009
months ahead.

00:08:01.009 --> 00:08:06.350
But sort of confidence and a belief that at
the end of the day, the Fed the Fed has your

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:07.350
back.

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:12.550
I guess the question is whether that means
anything for Main Street, as they like to

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:13.550
say.

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:17.770
Because at a quick glance, you see the unemployment
numbers and you see the stock market doing

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:18.770
OK.

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:19.770
It doesn't make sense.

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:23.590
Now, a lot of that is how bad was it expected
to get when people go on unemployment?

00:08:23.590 --> 00:08:28.380
They do presumably get some money which they
can spend, which could prop up stock market.

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:32.630
There are other factors, but how directly
does it relate to the average person's experience

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:33.849
and outlook?

00:08:33.849 --> 00:08:35.080
Not very directly at all.

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:36.080
Yeah.

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:37.349
I mean, that's that's the thing.

00:08:37.349 --> 00:08:46.300
I think investors really are mostly channeling
optimism about how far the Fed is going to

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:53.029
be willing to go to do whatever it takes to
protect the largest publicly traded companies

00:08:53.029 --> 00:08:55.100
and shareholders.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:58.270
Last thing, universal basic income.

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:02.410
There is a movement now, so universal basic
income, I guess as prominent as it has ever

00:09:02.410 --> 00:09:08.610
been, probably mostly thanks to Andrew Yang's
candidacy in the Democratic primary.

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:14.089
There are a lot of conversations now which
seem to go along the lines of, hey, we clearly

00:09:14.089 --> 00:09:19.750
can afford to do what Andrew Yang suggested
and we've clearly determined it's a good idea.

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:26.029
We being, I guess, you know, our legislators,
lawmakers, others saying there's really nothing

00:09:26.029 --> 00:09:27.029
here.

00:09:27.029 --> 00:09:29.140
These are extenuating emergency circumstances.

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:32.390
These are one time payments, even if it's
renewed one more time.

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:36.910
This is not universal basic income and it
does not affect the economy in the way that

00:09:36.910 --> 00:09:38.649
a UBI would.

00:09:38.649 --> 00:09:42.390
What can we glean about UBI from this, if
anything?

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:45.880
Well, I mean, the twelve hundred dollar check.

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:51.110
A lot of people are saying just keep that
going and that would be a form of, you know,

00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:52.420
a basic income payment.

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:56.860
Yeah, I get even with the twelve hundred dollar
check, there is means testing.

00:09:56.860 --> 00:10:01.700
Not everybody is going to receive that check
with the Yang proposal.

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:08.760
Remember that, you know, that proposal was
tied to a whole series of if then.

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:14.480
So if you want to opt into this program, then
check the math and make sure that if you're

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:18.981
receiving other forms of public support, if
you're on food stamps or, you know, there

00:10:18.981 --> 00:10:24.050
were a range of public programs that if you're
a beneficiary, you need to decide whether

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:30.360
you want to continue to receive those benefits
or let those benefits go and take essentially

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:36.370
what's behind door number two with although
I think later on it became changed to be additive,

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:38.460
although I don't know why you're not remembering.

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:39.460
No, no, no, no.

00:10:39.460 --> 00:10:45.200
It was additive in in the sense that if you're
not on any government program.

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:46.200
OK.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:50.779
You get it if you are on certain government
programs like Social Security.

00:10:50.779 --> 00:10:54.410
It was additive to that, but it wasn't additive
to the whole package.

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:55.410
Got it.

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:56.410
Good programs.

00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:57.410
OK.

00:10:57.410 --> 00:11:04.510
So but look, a basic income right now or an
income supplement to people makes perfect

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:05.510
sense to me.

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:09.839
Makes perfect sense to me in this moment that
you need to get cash into people's hands.

00:11:09.839 --> 00:11:10.839
You've got all these cars.

00:11:10.839 --> 00:11:16.390
You've got thousands and thousands of cars
of people, families lining up at the food

00:11:16.390 --> 00:11:17.390
banks.

00:11:17.390 --> 00:11:18.390
Right.

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:21.750
People aren't at the food bank because the
grocery store is out of food.

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:25.730
They're at the food bank because the bank
account is out of money.

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:34.410
And so in in this situation, direct payments
on a recurring basis for a period of time

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:37.040
make make perfect sense to me.

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:42.970
You don't necessarily think this tells us
anything about the long term idea of UBI?

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:47.590
No, not really, I mean, look, if we just did
this conversation where I said suppose we

00:11:47.590 --> 00:11:49.649
were in the fall of twenty nineteen.

00:11:49.649 --> 00:11:50.649
Yes.

00:11:50.649 --> 00:11:54.990
If we're in the fall of twenty nineteen and
we were talking about sending every person

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:57.510
in this country which there is a proposal.

00:11:57.510 --> 00:12:01.720
Congress, Congresswoman Rushford athlete has
a proposal to send.

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:04.150
I think it's two thousand dollars.

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:05.150
Right.

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:07.760
Every single person in this country for a
period of time.

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:13.760
Now that's a universal basic income payment
over a period of time.

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:19.190
Suppose we had talked about doing that in
the fall of last year when the unemployment

00:12:19.190 --> 00:12:21.460
rate was three and a half percent.

00:12:21.460 --> 00:12:26.420
And I think that most people would have said
there's your inflation invitation.

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:27.420
Right.

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:29.880
Because that economic environment.

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:31.040
People had jobs.

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:34.070
People were already have had income to spend.

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:38.970
And on top of that, you're going to layer
for eight thousand per household, depending

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:40.550
on the number of people in that.

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:46.279
I think it's a very different question in
that economic environment.

00:12:46.279 --> 00:12:50.240
We've been speaking with Stephanie Kelton,
professor of public policy and economics at

00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:51.800
Stony Brook University.

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:54.880
Stephanie, really appreciate your time once
again.

00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:55.560
Thanks for having me.

